      ":question/id": "3d80d2f6-dcf2-563b-a945-19e7cf78fdb1",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 27 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 11 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary row operations of the form $R_{i}:=R_{i}+kR_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary row operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make the entries above $a_{33}=2$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary row operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{1}:=R_{1}-R_{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 11 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\xrightarrow{R_{2}:=R_{2}+2R_{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 27 & 13 & 0 \\\\ 11 & 11 & 2\\end{bmatrix}=B\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary row operations are\n\n$R_{1}:=R_{1}-R_{3}$ and $R_{2}:=R_{2}+2R_{3}$.",
